
GDB Notes
---------

Explains how to use GDB to debug C programs

--------------------

Creating a debug file:

  gcc -g file.c -o file_debug

  -g     - produces debugging information in the OS¹s native format (stabs, COFF, XCOFF, or DWARF 2).
  -ggdb  - produces debugging information specifically intended for gdb.
  -ggdb3 - produces extra debugging information, for example: including macro definitions.
  -ggdb - by itself without specifying the level defaults to -ggdb2 (i.e., gdb for level 2).

Open the created debug file:

  gdb file_debug

--------------------- 

gdb commands:

  help			- get information about other commands
  help breakpoints

  set args arg1 arg2	- Set the arguments to be passed to the program being debugged

  run			- run the program

  list			- see the program, with line numbers

  break			- Set break points in the program
  break 5 		- Set break point at line 5
  break func_name 	- Set break points at the start of functions
  break if var == 8	- Can break based on conditionals

  del 5			- delete break points, in example if it was at line 5

  print			- print variables
  print var_name	- print a variable name
  print *var_name 	- see the value of pointer variable

  next 			- Go to the next 'active' line after a break point
			- next does NOT go into functions

  step  	    	- steps line by line in code, steps into function

  continue     		- Let the program finish, no more breakpoints

  finish		- Step out of a function

  frame			- Get the line number and current function

  info			- Show info
  info locals		- Show variable info
  info args		- Show arguments to the function
  info stack		- Show the current call stack

  watch var_name	- Watch a variable for if it changes
			- Lasts while the variable is in scope, i.e. variable scoped to a function


